$0086=255→0 caused $0087 to... no, $0087 was 96→90.\n\nHmm, what about $006E/$006F? Let me look at the watch again: $006F: 1,1,...,1 (48 samples), then 2,2,... So $006F went 1→2 at frame 49. Which frame did $0086 cross some value? At frame 49, $0086 ≈ 34. Hmm, irrelevant. What could $006F be? Maybe the number of \"objects\" or \"coins\". \n\n$006E stayed 1 the whole time.\n\nHmm, let me look at the initial read at the very start (x=40): I read address 128 length 16: [0,0,0,0,0,0,40,96,96,96,96,96,0,0,0,0] → $0080-$008B. $0086=40, $0087=96,$0088=96,$0089=96,$008A=96,$008B=96. At that time $006D/$006E/$006F unknown.\n\nAt x=221: [0,0,0,0,0,0,221,37,128,128,128,128,0,0,0,0] → $0087=37,$0088=128,$0089=128,$008A=128,$008B=128.\n\nInteresting: $0088-$008B change 96→128. These might be the player's \"screen\" or \"velocity\" values.\n\nHmm.\n\nOK let me take a step back and use a more reliable method to identify the canonical position: trace_writes. The game writes the canonical position variable every frame. If I watch writes to a candidate address $006E and see the values change with movement...\n\nActually the best: use play_input watch with a WIDER range of candidate addresses to identify which byte increases monotonically and wraps. But I already have $0086 doing that. The question is whether $0086 is canonical or derived.\n\nLet me test whether $0086 is canonical by a cleaner experiment: set up a state, then write $0086 to a value AND freeze/observe over several frames whether it stays. Earlier: wrote $0086=150, stepped 2 → stayed 150. Then wrote $006D=2, stepped 3 → $0086 became 69. So the reset to 69 happened after writing $006D. Maybe writing $006D caused a respawn, which resets position to 69 (the checkpoint/start-of-screen position). Hmm, 69 is odd for a start (normally 40). Actually 69 could be the position after the game \"clamped\" Mario to the camera edge (camera 69). \n\nLet me test writing $006D=1 alone (not 2) while keeping $0086, and see.\n\nActually, let me first just test: write $0086=150, step 5 frames, read $0086. If it stays 150 over 5 frames, then $0086 is canonical (not recomputed). Earlier I only stepped 2. Let me extend.\n\nActually more decisive: write $0086=200 and then step 10 frames and read. If canonical, stays ~200 (maybe +movement). If derived, reverts.\n\nLet me do that now. Current state: page 0, low 69, camera 69, Mario screen 0.\n\nWrite $0086=200, step 8, read $0086 and $006D and camera and OAM.\n\nHmm, but if Mario is at x=200 with camera 69, he'd be at screen 131; the Goomba at ~? could kill him within 8 frames. Risky but paused-steps allow me to check.\n\nActually, safer: write $0086=120 (screen x=51) away from the Goomba (Goomba at screen x~69? it's at 69-77 currently, Mario at 0). Writing Mario to 120 world = screen 51, still left of the Goomba at screen 69. Fine.\n\nHmm, but the point is to check persistence. Let me do $0086=200 and step 5.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole approach and use the SMB known truth: I'm now fairly confident that in SMB:\n- Player_X_Position = $0086? Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the SMB1 disassembly equates that I can partially recall... Actually, I recall these SMB RAM addresses quite specifically now from the \"SMB RAM map\":\n```\n$00CE  Player_Y_Position\n$00E4  Player_X_Position   <- ??? \n```\nNo. Hmm. Let me try to recall via the \"Player_PageLoc\" name: I believe Player_PageLoc = $006D? Hmm, or $006E?\n\nActually you know what, I recall the SMB player variables being:\n- $006D: Player_X_Position? \n- $006E: Player_PageLoc? \nNo...\n\nLet me just do the empirical test: write $0086=200, step many frames, and read $006D/$0086/$006E/$006F. That will reveal which byte the game maintains as the canonical x. If after several frames the position is preserved (200), $0086 is canonical. If it reverts to something, compare with $006D/$006E to see what the canonical is.\n\nLet me also watch a set with play_input but that's another round.\n\nDo it: write 200, step 