{"type":"thinking","text":"Again $006D reset to 0, $0086 to 114, camera 114. Mario at screen x=0. So the game DOES clamp the player to the camera's left edge whenever the page changes ahead.\n\nHmm. So the game clamps when the page jumps ahead? Wait, when the page is *hard-set* by me, the game resets the player to the camera's left edge. That's the SMB \"if player is too far from where the camera expects, snap\".\n\nSo writing $006D alone is not a stable way to move Mario.\n\nBUT: the natural wrap shows $006D increments while the camera stays behind. So during normal play, $006D can be one page ahead of the camera page. Why does my write reset it then?\n\nMaybe because I wrote $006D=2 while the camera page was 0, and the player's page should only be at most camera_page+1. Page 2 with camera page 0 is 2 ahead → invalid → reset.\n\nSo try writing $006D=1 when camera page is 0 and player page currently 1 — no change. Hmm.\n\nTo prove the page byte with a write, I could first *advance the camera* so it's at page 1, then write $006D=2. But that's a lot of walking.\n\nAlternative: Instead of setting an invalid page, I can demonstrate the page byte by writing it to a *lower* page (which the game accepts backward? no, clamp to camera edge).\n\nHmm. Actually, maybe the reset isn't a \"clamp\" but rather the game re-initializes the player's position each frame from the *camera edge* when the player is off-screen. Since writing page=2 puts Mario off-screen (screen x = 550-114 = 436 > 255), the engine's \"player is off-screen\" handler snaps him to the screen edge. That's plausible: SMB computes player screen x = player_x - screen_left; if > some max, it... hmm.\n\nSo the way to prove the page byte by write is to make sure the total stays on-screen. E.g., set $006D=1, $0086= 20 → x=276, camera 114 → screen x 162. On-screen. Then $006D=1 while $0086=20. Compare with $006D=0, $0086=20 → x=20, which is behind the camera (114) → clamps. Hmm.\n\nAlternatively: prove the page byte by