ly 1 page ahead of camera page 0, it might be allowed! Earlier the successful natural progression had page 1 while camera page 0. So page can be camera_page+1. Let's try $006D=1 with $0086=114 → x=370, which is camera+256, screen x = 256. The camera should scroll to keep Mario at screen ~112 → camera ~258 (which crosses into page 1!). So the camera will scroll forward and everything stays consistent.\n\nActually even better: set $006D=1, $0086=100 → x=356, screen x=242. Camera scrolls to 244. Forward scroll ~130. Might work.\n\nLet me try $006D=1, $0086=100. If it holds, the screenshot shows Mario at a new location (level x=356) with the same low byte 100, and camera ~244. Then compare to $006D=0, $0086=100 → x=100, behind camera 114 → clamp. Hmm, that comparison is confounded.\n\nThe clean proof: two states with same $0086:\n- A: $006D=0, $0086=100 → x=100 (behind camera 114 → clamps to 114).\n- B: $006D=1, $0086=100 → x=356 (valid, forward).\n\nThe screenshots differ (A: Mario at screen 0 near start-ish; B: Mario at screen ~112 in a different level area). Combined with RAM showing page 0 vs 1, it proves the page shifts him 256px.\n\nBut really, the strongest proof is the natural crossing diff: $0086 251→38 and $006D 0→1 in the same 40 frames while the visible level advanced continuously (no teleport). Plus, if I can get a write proof.\n\nLet me try $006D=1, $0086=100 now.\n\nActually, the current state: $006D=0, $0086=114, camera=114, Mario screen x 0. Let me write $006D=1, $0086=100 → x=356.\n\nStep 4, screenshot, read.\n\nLet me go."}
