{"type":"thinking","text":"Hmm! Writing $006D=1 reset it back to 0 and $0086 to 114 again. So the page jump of 1 (from camera page 0 with low 200 → x=456, screen x 342 off-screen) got snapped. Earlier with low=100 (x=356, screen 242, just within/near edge) it held. So the snap depends on how far off-screen the player is: screen x 242 is within the visible 256 range, so it held; screen x 342 is off-screen, so snapped.\n\nSo the game snaps the player when the computed screen x >= 256 (off right edge). That makes sense: SMB's player screen x is a byte (0-255); if page+low yields screen x > 255, the game can't represent it and re-initializes/scrolls.\n\nHmm, but the earlier natural crossing had page 1 while camera page 0, with screen x ~239 (within 255). So valid.\n\nSo a clean page-only A/B requires the camera to be positioned so both page values give on-screen x. That means the camera must be at least 256 behind. E.g., camera at 0: page0 low 200 → screen 200; page1 low 200 → screen 456 (off). Not possible either.\n\nThe only way to have both page 0 and page 1 valid with the same low byte is if the camera is < 256 and both x and x+256 map to 0..255 screen x — impossible since they differ by 256.\n\nSo a strict same-low A/B isn't physically possible. Instead, the proper page proof is the natural wrap demonstration: the same $006D/$0086 arithmetic and the fact that Mario continues walking right across the boundary without teleporting.\n\nAlternatively, prove the page byte by the write I did successfully: set page=1, low=100 (x=356) with camera at 114 → Mario drawn at screen x=242 (right edge). Before the write, page=0 low=114 (x=114) → screen x=0. The camera didn't move (114). So Mario's level position changed by +242 = +256 (page) − 14 (low). That arithmetic proves the page byte contributes 256.\n\nI think that's a solid proof. Let me also capture a screenshot where the camera has scrolled so the two states show clearly different scenery. Actually, I can now: after setting page=1, low=100 (x=356) successfully, the camera is 114 and Mario at screen 242. If I then walk right a bit (play_input right 30 frames), the camera will scroll to ~244 and Mario will be mid-screen at level ~400. Then the scenery is clearly a different part of the level. But that's just normal walking; the page proof stands from the RAM arithmetic.\n\nLet me redo the successful state and get a good screenshot: write $006D=1, $0086=100, step 4 (holds), screenshot (Mario at screen 242). That's the proof image.\n\nActually I already have that screenshot (5c350508) plus the read showing $006D=1,$0086=100,camera=114, OAM x=242. Good.\n\nBut the current state is now page 0, low 114 (after the failed attempt). Let me re-apply page=1, low=100 to have a consistent final state and maybe step frames to show scenery.\n\nActually, let me do a final, deliberate page-byte proof:\n1. Set page=1, low=100. Step 4. Screenshot (Mario right edge, camera 114). Read: $006D=1, $0086=100, camera 114, OAM x=242.\n2. Then play right 30 frames so the camera scrolls and Mario is mid-screen at a distinctly different level location; screenshot. Read camera.\n\nThis gives a nice visual: Mario far into the level.\n\nLet me do it.\n\nActually, thinking about it more: the \"proof\" for the page byte can also be done by showing that Mario's OAM screen x equals (page*256+low) - camera. With page=1, low=100, camera=114: 256+100-114 = 242. With page=0, low=114: 114-114=0. So the formula holds and the page contributes 256.\n\nGood. Let me re-apply and capture.\n\nAlso I should double-check the low byte with a clean write: set page=0, low=200 gave screen x=86 (OAM). And page=0 low=114 gave screen 0. That's the low-byte proof: writing 200 moved Mario to screen x=86 (from 0), camera fixed 114. Actually 200-114=86. Yes.\n\nWait, but when I wrote page=0/low=200, the screenshot showed Mario at screen x~86 — but I should confirm OAM. I didn't call list_sprites then. The screenshot showed him around x=86. Good enough, but let me get OAM for rigor.\n\nLet me now do a final clean set of proofs with OAM reads:\n\nProof 1 (low byte $0086): camera 114, page 0. Set $0086=114 → OAM x should be 0. Set $0086=200 → OAM x should be 86. Screenshot.\n\nActually current $0086=114. Let me write 200 and read OAM.\n\nProof 2 (page byte $006D): set $006D=1, $0086=100 → OAM x=242.\n\nLet me execute proof 1 now: write 134←200, step 2, OAM + screenshot + read.\n\nHmm, I already did this (screenshot f6eed8, but no OAM). Let me redo with OAM.\n\nActually let me combine: I'll do the writes and read OAM each time, and take screenshots. Three states:\nA) page0 low114: OAM 0 (already known from earlier: sprites x=0).\nB) page0 low200: OAM 86.\nC) page1 low100: OAM 242.\n\nLet me capture B and C with OAM + screenshots. Current is A ($0086=114, page0, camera 114).\n\nDo B: write 134←200, step 2, list_sprites + screenshot."}
