{"type":"text","text":"Mario's horizontal level position is a two-byte pair in World 1-1:\n\n## Addresses and encoding\n\n| Address | Meaning | Encoding |\n|---|---|---|\n| **`$006D`** | Horizontal position **page** (high byte) | binary 0–255 |\n| **`$0086`** | Horizontal position **within the page** (low byte) | binary 0–255 |\n\n**Absolute level x = `$006D` × 256 + `$0086`** (e.g. World 1-1 starts at `$006D`=0, `$0086`=40=0x28). On-screen sprite x = absolute x − camera (`$071C`, page `$071A`; `$073F` is the NMI's scroll byte).\n\n## How each was found and proven\n\n- **`$0086` (low byte)** — Differential search: three walking rounds (diff `increased` left 172 → 57 → 22 survivors) kept `$0086`, rising ~1.5 px/frame. A 60-frame `play_input` watch showed it climb 244→255, **wrap to 0**, and continue to 49 while the camera (`$073F`) only reached 66 — proving a separate page byte exists.\n  - **Write proof:** paused, wrote `$0086`=150 (was 69), stepped 2 frames → `list_sprites` showed Mario's OAM entries move from screen x=0 to **x=81** with the camera still 69 (screenshot: Mario further right, next to the first Goomba). A second write `$0086`=200 with camera 114 put his OAM at x=86 = 200−114.\n\n- **`$006D` (page byte)** — The watch showed `$006D` tick **0→1 on the exact frame `$0086` wrapped 255→0**. A snapshot diff across a real crossing reproduced it: `$006D` 0→1 with `$0086` 251→38 while the camera was still on page 0 at 113 — so it's the player's page, not the camera's.\n  - **Write proof:** with the camera fixed at 114 and `$0086`=200 (page 0), Mario's OAM x read 86; after `write_memory $006D`=1 with `$0086`=100 and stepping, RAM kept `$006D`=1 / `$0086`=97 and Mario's OAM x jumped to **239 = (256+97)−114** (screenshot: Mario at the screen's right edge — +256 level pixels despite a *lower* low byte).\n\n## Caveat found while proving\n\nIf a hand-written page puts his computed screen x outside 0–255 (more than one page ahead of the camera), the engine snaps Mario back to the camera's left edge — so the page proof uses a one-page step. This is why writing page=2 while the camera was on page 0 reverted, and it's recorded in the notes.\n\nAll three discoveries (`0x0086`, `0x006D`, and the encoding note) are recorded with `source: \"differential search\"`, and `promote_mappings` saved them to the ROM's mapping file (now 12 addresses, 14 tiles, 7 palettes, 1 sprite)."}
